# Start with your first trace

Uncoil connects the model calls, tool calls, prompts, costs, failures, and evaluation signals inside an agent run. This guide takes you from an empty project to one useful trace in the dashboard. Plan on about five minutes.

You need an Uncoil project and its API key. The key belongs to one region, EU or US, so create the project in the region where you want your data stored. See [Choose an Uncoil Cloud region](/docs/data-regions) if you are unsure.

## 1. Install the SDK

```bash
npm install uncoil-sdk
```

Using Python? Install `uncoil-sdk` with pip instead and follow the [Python SDK guide](/docs/python) for the matching calls.

```bash
pip install uncoil-sdk
```

## 2. Set your API key

Keep the key in a server-side environment variable. The SDK reads `UNCOIL_API_KEY` automatically, so you do not need to pass it in code.

```bash
export UNCOIL_API_KEY=uncoil_sk_live_...
```

> Never put a project API key in browser code. If the variable is missing, the TypeScript SDK prints a `UNCOIL_API_KEY is not set` warning and records nothing, and the Python SDK raises a `ValueError` when the client is created.

## 3. Wrap one run

`traceAgent` turns every call of your function into one trace. Each call gets a new run, and everything you record inside it hangs from that run.

```ts
import { traceAgent, span } from "uncoil-sdk";

export const answer = traceAgent(
  async (question: string) => {
    const docs = await span("search-docs", () => searchDocs(question), {
      type: "tool",
      input: question,
    });

    return span(
      "chat",
      async (step) => {
        const reply = await callModel(question, docs);
        step.set({
          model: "your-model-id",
          inputTokens: reply.usage.input,
          outputTokens: reply.usage.output,
        });
        return reply.text;
      },
      { type: "llm", input: question },
    );
  },
  { name: "support-agent", environment: "development", release: "quickstart" },
);

await answer("Where is my order?");
```

Replace `searchDocs` and `callModel` with your own functions. If you already use the OpenAI or Anthropic SDK, you can skip the manual `span` calls and wrap the client once instead. See [OpenAI, Anthropic and LangGraph](/docs/provider-integrations).

## 4. Verify the result

Open the project dashboard and select the newest run. A healthy first trace has:

- a root run named after your agent,
- one child span per `span` you recorded, in the order they ran,
- timing on every span, plus the model and token counts you set,
- the input and output of each step, with obvious secrets already removed.

If the run does not appear within a minute, open [Troubleshoot your first trace](/docs/troubleshooting).

## What to do next

- Learn the full SDK surface in the [TypeScript SDK](/docs/typescript) or [Python SDK](/docs/python) guide.
- Read [what is in a trace](/docs/trace-anatomy) so you know what each field means.
- Attach [feedback and scores](/docs/feedback-and-scores) to runs so quality shows up beside cost and latency.
